\frac{V_{k_{2x},S,-k_{1x}}}{\nomvk1\omega_{S,k_{2y}-k_{1y}}}e^{-\sigma^2(k_{0x}-k_{1x})^2-\sigma^2(k_{0y}-k_{1y})^2-\sigma_0^{2}(k_{2y}-k_{1y})^2}\nonumber\\
&&\ \ \ \ \ \ \times e^{ix_{\tau}(k_{0x}-k_{1x})+i(y_{\tau}-y_\tau^S)(k_{0y}-k_{1y})}.\nonumber
\eea

In the support of the Gaussian, we have argued that $\vk_1\sim \vk_0$.  We kept the linear correction in the exponential, as it is multiplied by a large time.  However, in the $\omega$ terms in the denominator, there is no such large time and so we may simply consider the constant term in the expansion.  The same is true for the interaction $V_{kSk}$ because it has support at $|x|\sim O(m)$ and so it varies slowly with respect to $k_{1x}$.  These approximations are exact in our large $\sigma$ limit, and lead to
\beq
