  \label{fig:many-noinputs-bcfix-pre}
  %Fig 9.12
\commentAlt{Figure~\ref{fig:many-noinputs-bcfix-pre}: 
Same graph as in Figure~\ref{fig:many-noinputs}, but with nodes colored as follows: 1 is green, 2 is yellow, 3 is dark blue, 4 is light blue, 5 is brown, 6, 7 are red.
}
\end{figure}

We have discussed, for simplicity, only the case of a homogeneous graph on which we impose a typing that takes the no-input case into account. This idea can be easily extended to the typed case in a straightforward way. Nodes can be assigned the same color only if they have the same node type and the same number of
input edges of each type. Not all such colorings are balanced---correspond to fibrations---but all balanced colorings must have this property since
fibrations have the lifting property, and in this generalization, they must also preserve node and edge types.




