Next, we investigate the effects of the other coupling coefficients, $D_y, D_z,$ and $D_w$, on the validity of the theory. Based on the derivation, we expect that the analytical framework to become invalid when $D_y$ or $D_z$ is non-negligible. In contrast, our theory predicts that increasing $D_w$ does not affect the results. To test these expectations, we perform numerical simulations using the coupled hyperchaotic Lorenz system (Figs.~\ref{fig:hyperchaotic_Lorenz_Dy_vary}, \ref{fig:hyperchaotic_Lorenz_Dz_vary}, and \ref{fig:hyperchaotic_Lorenz_Dw_vary}).
