\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
The ql-ROM is first demonstrated in Section~\ref{sec:resKS} on the Kuramoto–Sivashinsky (KS) equation in both bursting and chaotic regimes. In Section~\ref{sec:resKOL}, the method is demonstrated on the 
two-dimensional Navier-Stokes equations (Kolmogorov flow).
\subsection{Kuramoto–Sivashinsky equation}\label{sec:resKS}
The KS equation, \eqref{eq:ks}, has different nonlinear dynamics for different values of the parameters $L$ and $\nu$. In bursting regime, the solution evolves intermittently between pseudo-steady cellular states of opposite sign \cite{Floryan2022}. When projected onto the leading spatial Fourier modes, the system oscillates between two saddle points connected by four heteroclinic orbits. The solution has six distinct regions in the geometry of the  manifold, suggesting a natural choice of $K=6$ clusters, as shown in the left panel of Figure~\ref{fig:KSbursting}. 
