Existing studies often oversimplify PA systems, limiting the accuracy and effectiveness of resource allocation strategies. This work proposes a more generalized model~\cite{ding2025flexible, shan2025exploiting, hu2025, xu2025pinching, shan2025multigroup}, which encompasses multiple dielectric waveguides incorporating the beamforming design at the BS, where each PA can independently and dynamically adjust its positions and allocated transmission powers, considering waveguide propagation losses. As depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:power},
%The magnitude of power fed by the BS into the feed point of the $n$-th waveguide is $|w_n|^2$ and 
the propagation loss between two consecutive PAs, e.g., the $m$-th and ($m-1$)-th PA, in the $n$-th waveguide is denoted as ``PL($d^{n,m}_{n,m-1}$)'' with their spatial separation $d_{n,m-1}^{n,m} = x_{n,m}^{\rm Pin} - x_{n,m-1}^{\rm Pin}$.
The power allocated to the $m$-th PA in the $n$-th waveguide is denoted by $p_{n,m}$, which can be dynamically managed\footnote{%Matching the effective refractive indices of the waveguide and the PA make it possible to radiate full power of a waveguide from a single PA with a coupling length of $\frac{\pi}{2 \kappa}$, where $\kappa \in \mathbb{R}$ denotes the coupling coefficient that affcated by choices of waveguide types. Recent studies have demonstrated the feasibility of enabling dynamic control over the power extraction ratio by adjusting the coupled dielectric segment~\cite{wang2025modeling}. Thus, the power distribution of a waveguide can be precisely regulated by varying the coupling length of each PA by adopting the auto scaling function.
The power extracted by each PA depends on its coupling length to the waveguide~\cite{wang2025modeling, okamoto2021fundamentals}. To abstract from specific hardware implementations, we assume that PAs dynamically adjust their coupling lengths via an auto-scaling mechanism, enabling precise control of the extracted power.} as well as its spatial position\footnote{Given the small physical length of PAs relative to practical waveguides~\cite{okamoto2021fundamentals}, their impact on propagation loss is neglected.}, subject to constraints $0 \leq x_{n,m}^{\rm{Pin}} \leq L, d_{n,m-1}^{n,m} \geq \gamma$, where $\gamma > 0$ denotes the minimum allowable spacing among PAs to mitigate coupling effects. Considering lossy dielectric waveguides~\cite{hu2025} and input power $|w_n|^2, \forall n$, at the feeding point of the $n$-th waveguide, the maximum available power at the $m$-th PA of the $n$-th waveguide, $\forall n,m$, can be mathematically modeled as follows:
\begin{align}\label{eq:power}
p^{\rm{max}}_{n,m} \hspace{-1mm}= &|w_n|^2\hspace{-0.2mm} e^{-2 \alpha \hspace{-0.2mm} \left\|\hspace{-0.3mm}\bm{\psi}_n^{\rm{FP}}\hspace{-0.7mm}-\bm{\psi}_{n,m}^{\rm{Pin}}\hspace{-0.6mm} \right\|} \hspace{-1mm}-\hspace{-1.9mm} \sum_{t=1}^{m-1} \hspace{-1mm} p_{n,t} e^{-2\alpha\hspace{-0.3mm}\left\|\hspace{-0.4mm}\bm{\psi}_{n,m}^{\rm{Pin}} \hspace{-0.5mm}- \bm{\psi}_{n,t}^{\rm{Pin}}\hspace{-0.5mm}\right\|}, \hspace{-0.5mm}
\end{align}
where $\epsilon_r, \text{tan}\left(\delta_e\right),\alpha = \lambda_g \epsilon_r \pi f_c^2c^{-2} \text{tan}\left(\delta_e\right)\in \mathbb{R}^+$ are dielectric constant, effective electric loss tangent of the dielectric waveguide, and the attenuation constant~\cite{hu2025, balanis2024balanis}, respectively.
%Consequently, this work is not only optimised the power allocated to each waveguide $z_n$, more importantly, we aim to optimiza the the transmitted power at each PA, $p_{n,m}, \forall n, m,$ can be adaptively managed rather than following the aussmption of equal power distribution in same waveguide, offering an additional degree of freedom for system design and optimization.
%the proposed generalized model explicitly characterizes the interplay among waveguide propagation effects, phase shifts, PA positioning, and beamforming design. As indicated by~\eqref{eq:power}, 
Thus, the maximum allowable power for each PA is jointly influenced by its position along the waveguide and the power allocated to preceding PAs. 
%This distinction sets the proposed approach apart from existing studies, which commonly assume equal power distribution among PAs and disregard waveguide propagation losses. 
In particular, the introduced model inherently captures critical trade-offs: the balance between waveguide propagation losses and the path losses from PAs to users, and power allocation among the PAs. These comprehensive considerations significantly enhance the practical relevance and effectiveness of subsequent optimization.
