\subsection{Channel Model and Performance Metric}
The proposed system assumes a static channel model with perfect channel state information at the BS~\cite{wang2025modeling,hu2025,shan2025exploiting}. The wireless channel between the $M$ PAs on the $n$-th waveguide and the $k$-th user, $\textbf{h}_{n,u_{k}} \hspace{-2.3mm} \in \hspace{-1.4mm} \mathbb{C}^{\hspace{-0.4mm}M \hspace{-0.4mm} \times \hspace{-0.4mm}1}\hspace{-0.9mm}$, is modeled by exploiting the spherical-wave near-field channel model as~\cite{hu2025}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:channel_user}
\textbf{h}_{n,u_{k}} \hspace{-1.5mm}\left(\hspace{-0.3mm}\textbf{x}^{\rm{Pin}}_{n}\hspace{-0.7mm}\right) \hspace{-1.2mm}=\hspace{-1.3mm} \left[\hspace{-0.7mm}\tfrac{\eta^{\hspace{-0.6mm}\frac{1}{2}} \hspace{-0.6mm} e^{\hspace{-0.6mm}-j\hspace{-0.4mm}\frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \hspace{-0.7mm} \left\|\hspace{-0.4mm} \bm{\phi}_k \hspace{-0.35mm}- \hspace{-0.13mm}\bm{\psi}^{\rm{Pin}}_{n,1}\hspace{-0.6mm}\right\|} }{\left\|\bm{\phi}_k - \bm{\psi}^{\rm{Pin}}_{n,1}\right\|}\hspace{-0.7mm}, ..., \hspace{-0.7mm}\tfrac{\eta^{\hspace{-0.6mm}\frac{1}{2}} \hspace{-0.6mm} e^{\hspace{-0.6mm}-j\hspace{-0.4mm}\frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \hspace{-0.7mm} \left\|\hspace{-0.4mm} \bm{\phi}_k \hspace{-0.35mm}- \hspace{-0.13mm}\bm{\psi}^{\rm{Pin}}_{n,M}\hspace{-0.6mm}\right\|} }{\left\|\bm{\phi}_k - \bm{\psi}^{\rm{Pin}}_{n,M}\right\|}\hspace{-0.7mm}\right]^T \hspace{-1.5mm}, \ \forall n,
\end{equation}
where $\textbf{x}^{\rm{Pin}}_{n} \triangleq \{x_{n,1}^{\rm{Pin}}, ..., x_{n,M}^{\rm{Pin}}\}$ denotes the set of PA locations on the $n$-th waveguide and $\eta = \frac{c^2}{16\pi^2f_c^2}$ with $c, f_c,$ and $ \lambda$ representing the light speed, carrier frequency, and wavelength in free space, respectively. 
%Since there are $M$ PAs positioned along the same waveguide, the signals transmitted by different PAs differ primarily by their relative phase shifts~\cite{ding2025flexible}. 
%To facilitate both analysis and resource allocation design, we decompose the waveguide propagation into two distinct components to explicitly capture the corresponding power attenuation and phase variations, respectively. Accordingly, 
The  associated phase-shift vector, excluding propagation losses, is defined as~\cite{bereyhi2025downlink, ding2025flexible}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:channel_prop}
\textbf{g}_n\hspace{-1mm} \left(\hspace{-0.5mm}\textbf{x}^{\rm{Pin}}_{n}\hspace{-0.5mm}\right)\hspace{-1mm}=\hspace{-1mm} \left[\hspace{-0.3mm}e^{-j\frac{2\pi}{\lambda_g}\hspace{-0.5mm}\left\|\hspace{-0.4mm}\bm{\psi}_n^{\rm{FP}} \hspace{-0.8mm}-\hspace{-0.2mm} \bm{\psi}_{n,1}^{\rm{Pin}}\hspace{-0.6mm}\right\|}, ..., e^{-j\frac{2\pi}{\lambda_g}\hspace{-0.5mm}\left\|\hspace{-0.4mm}\bm{\psi}_n^{\rm{FP}} \hspace{-0.8mm}-\hspace{-0.2mm} \bm{\psi}_{n,M}^{\rm{Pin}}\hspace{-0.6mm}\right\|}\hspace{-0.3mm}\right]^{\hspace{-0.5mm}T}\hspace{-0.8mm}, \ \forall n,
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_g = \frac{\lambda}{\eta_{\mathrm{eff}}}$,$\lambda = \frac{2\pi}{f_c}$ and $\eta_{\mathrm{eff}} > 1$ represents the effective refractive index~\cite{ding2025flexible}. 
The propagation losses within the waveguide are separately modeled by leveraging a diagonal power allocation matrix $\mathbf{P}_n \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times M}$, whose diagonal entries $\sqrt{p_{n,m}}, \forall n,m$, representing the amplitude scaling factor applied to the corresponding PA. The transmission power $p_{n,m}$ is constrained by a maximum allowable power budget after waveguide propagation loss, denoted by $p^{\max}_{n,m}$, as shown in~\eqref{eq:power}.

To simplify notation, we define the equivalent channel vector from the $N$ waveguides to the $k$-th user, $\forall k$, as:
\begin{equation}
\bm{\beta}_k \hspace{-1mm}=\hspace{-1mm} \left[\textbf{h}_{1,u_{k}}^T \hspace{-1.5mm} \left(\hspace{-0.5mm}\textbf{x}^{\rm{Pin}}_{1}\hspace{-0.5mm}\right)\hspace{-1mm}\textbf{P}_1 \textbf{g}_1 \hspace{-1.2mm} \left(\hspace{-0.5mm}\textbf{x}^{\rm{Pin}}_{1}\hspace{-0.5mm}\right)\hspace{-1MM}, ...,  \textbf{h}_{N,u_{k}}^T \hspace{-1.5mm} \left(\hspace{-0.5mm}\textbf{x}^{\rm{Pin}}_{N}\hspace{-0.5mm}\right)\hspace{-1mm}\textbf{P}_N \textbf{g}_N \hspace{-1.2mm} \left(\hspace{-0.5mm}\textbf{x}^{\rm{Pin}}_{N}\hspace{-0.5mm}\right)\right]^{\hspace{-0.3mm}T}\hspace{-1.2mm}.
\end{equation}
Then, the received signal at the $k$-th user can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}\label{rec_signal}
r_k = \bm{\beta}_k^H \textbf{w} s+ \epsilon_k, \ \forall k, 
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon_k \sim \mathcal{CN}\left(0, \sigma_{k}^2\right)$ is the additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN) at the $k$-th user and $\sigma_{k}^2$ denotes its noise power.
Thus, the achievable data rate for the $k$-th user can be written as:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rate}
R_k = \log_2\left(1 + \tfrac{\left|\bm{\beta}_k^H \textbf{w}\right|^2}{\sigma_k^2}\right), \quad \forall k.
\end{equation}
