Assessing the quality of communicative multi-agent systems has become an important research area, particularly for developing agents capable of understanding and exchanging multimodal signals. COMMA \cite{COMMA}, for example, provides a benchmark that evaluates the communicative effectiveness of multimodal multi-agent systems through coordinated visual and textual tasks. In contrast, BattleAgentBench \cite{BattleAgentBench} introduces a mixed cooperative-adversarial environment, allowing for the evaluation of both collaboration and competition among LLMs in multi-agent scenarios. For cooperative agents, CH-MARL \cite{CH-MARL} introduces a benchmark involving agents with heterogeneous sensors and action spaces, designed to emulate the diversity and complexity of real-world multi-agent environments. This enables the evaluation of agents’ coordination, adaptability, and communication across varied modalities and capabilities. Similarly, VS-Bench \cite{VS-Bench} focuses on vision-language models (VLMs), testing their strategic reasoning and long-term planning skills within shared tasks, thereby pushing the limits of what VLMs can achieve in collaborative agent settings. \\

Recent evaluation frameworks have emphasized core components of agent rationality, including coherence in decision-making, concise communication, and utility-optimized interaction strategies \cite{Rationality_LLMs_Agents}. A broader review of multimodal language model evaluation identifies persistent challenges such as aligning information across modalities, generalizing to novel tasks, and maintaining response consistency in dynamic interaction contexts \cite{Evaluation_MLLMs}. LLaVA-Critic \cite{LLaVA-Critic} introduces a model-based evaluation framework that incorporates feedback loops to assess multimodal output quality in an interactive and supervised manner. Collectively, these emerging benchmarks and evaluation strategies signal a shift toward more nuanced, standardized, and context-sensitive assessments reflecting the increasing complexity and interactivity of modern multimodal multi-agent systems.

