Table \ref{stp:interventions} shows the different intervention schemes we investigated in this simulation study. As static schemes are the most prevalent in research, we use the corresponding CDRC as a benchmark to illustrate how a switch to an MTP changes both estimand and estimability. The causal parameter of interest is here  $\psi(P_0) = \mathrm{E}\left(Y^{A=x}\right)$. The next scheme we considered is a naive shift and its naive shift-response curve. Here, we estimate $\psi(P_0) = \mathrm{E}\left(Y^{A=a+x}\right)$. It is the simplest type of MTP. For the naive shift, we also considered its stochastic variant, namely $\psi(P_0) =\mathrm{E}\left(Y^{A\sim\mathrm{N}(a+x, \sigma^2)}\right)$ and $\mathrm{E}\left(Y^{A\sim\mathrm{N}(a+x, \sigma^2)}\right)$, where the intervention scheme remains the same on average, but has a random component added. In addition to these rather simple MTPs we examined a threshold MTP and a shift MTP (in order to differentiate this scheme from the naive shift, we will refer to this as a conditional shift MTP) as the two highlighted schemes by D\'{i}az et al.\ \cite{diaz2023nonparametric}. The threshold shift MTP only shifts observations below a certain threshold with estimate $\psi(P_0) =\mathrm{E} \left(Y^{A =\max(x, a)}\right)$. The conditional shift MTP only shifts observations where a specific function is fulfilled, i.\ e., $\mathrm{E}\left(Y^{A=a+x\mathbbm{1}_{a+x\leq w} }\right)$. For the function $w(a)$, we simply used the maximum and minimum values (depending on the direction of the shift) occurring in the data for each given $L$, meaning no observations will be shifted to have a treatment value which exceeds the treatment range of the observed data of their $L$ group. We estimated shift-response curves for both of these. As we do not believe stochastic versions of either of these to be useful, we did not include them in the study.



For each of the $3$ positivity situations, the dose-response curve was fitted using the CICI package with a g-formula based on a generalized GAM. Additionally, we used a doubly robust method based on Kennedy \cite{kennedy2017non}. All MTP models were fitted with the R package lmtp's Sequential Doubly Robust Estimator \cite{williams2023lmtp}, as the authors recommended method, used two folds for cross-fitting, and the following algorithms in its Superlearners: glm, gam, glmnet, and earth.
