%_____________________________Qaim's intro_______________________________
Nuclear medicine is a rapidly developing field in which radionuclides are used both for diagnostics and therapeutics. 
Nuclear medicine can be used to diagnose a variety of diseases such as cardiac infarction, bone disorders, gallbladder dysfunction, and cancer \cite{US_health_nuc_med}. 
Cancer is one of the leading causes of death worldwide, accounting for almost one in six deaths, resulting in nearly $10$ million deaths globally in 2022 \cite{WHO_cancer}. 
New advancements in diagnostics and treatment can enable more patients to survive cancer.


Currently, the most commonly-used tracer for positron emission tomography (PET) studies is $^{18}$F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), a glucose analog \cite{Trotter2023PositronPET/CT}. 
However, this tracer does not specifically target cancer cells, although the uptake of glucose is typically higher in those cells.
It is thus only a qualitative diagnostic tool. 
For cell targeting, a different class of radiopharmaceuticals, mostly based on radionuclides of metallic nature, with versatile chemistry is needed. 
These can be attached to ligands like DOTA \cite{Trotter2023PositronPET/CT}, thereby enabling the labeling of antibodies, peptides and other larger targeting moieties. 
Another important factor is quantification of the dose. 
This is generally done via a PET measurement prior to therapy.
This necessitates the development  of a new generation of non-standard PET tracers with half-lives suitable for these applications (generally ranging between $12$ hours and several days) as well as therapeutic radionuclides with the same or similar chemistry. 
This methodology, termed today as the \enquote{theranostic approach}, was first applied using the isotopic pair $^{86}$Y/$^{90}$Y, whereby $^{86}$Y ($t_{1/2} = 14.74$ h) is a positron emitter and $^{90}$Y ($t_{1/2} = 2.7$ d) is a $\beta^-$-emitting therapeutic radionuclide (for historical development, see \cite{Herzog1993MeasurementRadiotherapeutics, Rosch2017The90Y}). %there are some arrows and stuff here in his notes which are a bit uclear to me
In the meantime, several other pairs have been developed \cite{Qaim2019TheranosticMethodologies}.
However, the pair $^{86}$Y/$^{90}$Y still remains very interesting, mainly because of its stable trivalent chemistry, although $^{86}$Y is not an ideal radionuclide for PET investigations.
The total $\beta^+$-emission intensity in its decay amounts only to $27.2 \pm 2.0 \%$ \cite{Gula2020State-of-the-artImaging, Uddin2022PositronStudies} and a large number of low and high energy $\gamma$-rays are emitted which adversely  affect the resolution of PET scans. 
Nonetheless, through application of several corrections \cite{Herzog2008, Walrand2011DosimetryImaging} it is possible to use this radionuclide in PET studies which can be utilized to monitor the uptake of $^{90}$Y in patients, enabling personalized dosing and enhancing treatment efficiency.

The therapeutic radionuclide $^{90}$Y is commercially available through the $^{90}\text{Sr} \xrightarrow{\beta^-}$$^{90}\text{Y}$ generator system \cite{international2009iaea}. 
The positron emitter $^{86}$Y has also been developed up to the stage of clinical scale production but still further work is in progress.
Several reaction routes have been investigated for its production (for reviews, see \cite{Rosch2017The90Y, Uddin2025An86Srd2n-reactions}). 
In this work we have investigated a possible pathway for  $^{86}$Y production, namely $^{\text{nat}}$Zr($d$,$x$)$^{86}$Y, for which the existing database of reaction data is not strong. 
This work is part of a larger international project aiming to study multiple production pathways for $^{86}$Y, including $^{86}$Sr($p$,$n$), $^{86}$Sr($d$,$2n$) and $^{\text{nat}}$Zr($d$,$x$) reactions.
Results for the other production routes have already been recently published \cite{Uddin2020Accurate86Srpn-reaction, Uddin2022ExcitationY, Uddin2024ExcitationSr}.
Compared to strontium, zirconium is less reactive and has a higher melting point, making it a more preferable target material.
The $^{\text{nat}}$Zr($d$,$x$) production route may also serve as a proof-of-concept for future measurements using enriched Zr-targets.

Besides the search for a possible new route for the production of the medically important positron emitter $^{86}$Y, there was another motivation behind our studies, namely to test the predictive power of nuclear model calculations. 
It is known that the modeling of the activation products formed in the interaction of deuterons with target nuclei is more challenging than with proton beams \cite{Uddin2024ExcitationSr, Bem2009LowAl27, Avrigeanu2016Deuteron-inducedMeV, CERN-Proceedings-2019-001}. 
Here we present detailed cross section measurements on $^{\text{nat}}$Zr($d$,$x$)$^{86m,g, 87m,g, 88}$Y and several other reaction products up to a deuteron energy of $50$ MeV. 
The experimental data are compared with results of several nuclear model code calculations, and the results shed some light on the success of model calculations in reproducing the experimental data.


