\subsection{Physical yields}
\label{sec:physical_yields}

Figure \ref{fig:yield_Zr} shows the physical yields for all the different yttrium isotopes produced in the $^\text{{nat}}$Zr($d$,$x$) reactions as a function of deuteron beam energy. 
These yields were calculated using the recommendations in \cite{Otuka2015DefinitionsYields}, linearly interpolating between the cross sections reported in this work.
We can see that the yield for the production of $^{86}$Y is higher than the yields for all other yttrium isotopes produced, and that the main contaminants are $^{90\text{m}}$Y and $^{87}$Y. 
We also see that to maximize $^{86}$Y production compared to the other yttrium isotopes, higher energy beams should be employed.
The optimal energy window is therefore apparently approximately $40$ MeV and above. 
Over the energy range of $E_d=50\rightarrow 40$ MeV, for example, the yield of $^{86}$Y would be $47$ MBq for an irradiation at $1 \mu$A for $1$ hour, but the impurity level would be appreciable (approximately $35$\% $^{90\text{m}}$Y, $7$\% $^{87}$Y and $0.3$\% $^{88}$Y). 
The impurity $^{90\text{m}}$Y would likely not be a major problem because it is short-lived. 
The long-lived $^{87}$Y impurity, however, may be perilous. 

