Figure \ref{fig:yield_routes} shows the physical yield for producing $^{86}$Y, as a comparison between different production routes.
The data used for the $^{\text{nat}}$Zr($d$,$x$)$^{86}$Y reaction are from this work. The data for the $^{86}$Sr($d$,$2n$)$^{86}$Y reaction are from a recent publication under this collaboration \cite{Uddin2025An86Srd2n-reactions}, and for the $^{86}$Sr($p$,$n$)$^{86}$Y reaction we have plotted both the data from the recent publication under this collaboration \cite{Uddin2025An86Srd2n-reactions} and data from the IAEA. All other data are from the IAEA \cite{InternationalAtomicEnergyAgencyIAEA2001ChargedProject, Tarkanyi2019RecommendedEmitters, Hermanne2021UpgradeIsotopes, Hermanne2023EvaluatedPET}.
We can see that production through $^{86}$Sr($p$,$n$) gives the highest yield for energies up to about $25$ MeV and is very suitable for production of $^{86}$Y in the low energy range.
As seen in Ref. \cite{Uddin2025An86Srd2n-reactions}, the recent data from Uddin \emph{et al.} clearly shows the impact of having adopted rather wrong old experimental data in the most recent IAEA evaluation for the $^{86}$Sr($p$,$n$)$^{86}$Y reaction.
Between $25$ and $30$ MeV the yield for the $^{86}$Sr($d$,$2n$) reaction becomes higher, though the greater radionuclidic impurity for this pathway makes the proton route the more attractive option for production of clinically-relevant $^{86}$Y \cite{Uddin2025An86Srd2n-reactions}. 
Above $40$ MeV, the $^{88}$Sr($p$,$3n$) route gives the highest yield for production of $^{86}$Y. 
Production through $^\text{{nat}}$Zr($d$,$x$)$^{86}$Y gives the lowest yield among all the production pathways.
However, comparing yields obtained with enriched targets to those obtained with natural composed targets is somewhat unjustified, given the proof-of-concept nature of this measurement. 
Further investigations need to be performed to measure and calculate the yields for $^{90}$Zr($d$,$\alpha 2n$)$^{86}$Y, to compare against the established routes. 
