thoughts on math finance

    Simple theory/simple implementation - pairs trading. Find two similar stocks A and B that tend to move together. Estimate the long-term average ratio of their prices A/B. If the current ratio of prices is 10\% higher than the long-term average, short A and buy B. If the current ratio of the prices is 10\% lower than the long-term average, buy A and short B. In both cases take off the position when the ratio crosses 1. This is a technical strategy with obvious fundamental reasoning and simple to execute.

    Simple/Complex - statistical arbitrage. Same basic theory as pairs trading, but done using a computer to sift through all stocks and find 200 with prices below the average of similar stocks and 200 to short with prices above the average of similar stocks—carefully balanced to eliminate industry, interest rate and other risks. Just as simple in theory as pairs trading, but much more complex to execute.

    Complex/simple - volatility arbitrage. Buy options trading at implied volatility lower than the historical volatility of the underlying, write similar options trading at implied volatility higher than the historical volatility of the underlying. The mathematical theory underlying the strategy is complex, but the implementation is very simple.

    Complex/compex - fixed income arbitrage. Build a volatility surface of interest rates over the future and construct a large, highly levered portfolio of fixed-income securities, futures, swaps and options that locks in a small profit with very small risk. This is complex in both theory and execution.

