Comparing the TPR and TNR of our model with those of López-Cano we see that our U-Net is rather conservative in predicting protohaloes.
Our NN achieves only 73\% TPR, while the TNR is extremely high with 96\%. This shows that our CNN approach is very accurate in predicting which particles will not end up in a halo. The overall accuracy of the U-Net is also very high with just below 91\%, which is better than the results from the other papers. However, our model is slightly worse than that of López-Cano et al. in terms of the F$_1$-score, which is more representative with unbalanced datasets, but still performs better than ExSHalos. Nonetheless, our classification network proves to be a fairly accurate and competitive model for identifying protohalo patches from the initial density field. 

