\libname{BurTorch} contributes to the discussion on ML systems and improved implementations, emphasizing the importance of language diversification, particularly in resource-constrained environments.

The widespread use of Python in Machine Learning is largely driven by research needs. Traditionally, compile-based languages have two major drawbacks. First, extensive compilation times are required to optimize performance when building from source, with large projects often taking hours to compile. Second, as models grow in complexity, designing algorithms closer to hardware becomes increasingly time-consuming. A common approach has been to introduce more abstractions. However, there may be an alternative path. By leveraging smaller code sizes, the machine learning community can explore compile-based languages as a more efficient alternative.

\paragraph{Bridging the gap between academic and industry research.}

Academic research often requires significant additional implementation efforts when applied in industry. The bridge between academia and industry remains largely implicit, with notable gaps. For example, when applying specific algorithms for societal benefit, researchers in the industry may be unable to share their insights with academia due to internal restrictions and the lack of formal channels.

One approach is to establish bridges between research and industry in various forms. However, \libname{BurTorch} offers an alternative. Instead of focusing on direct integration, \libname{BurTorch} advocates for leveraging a compact codebase in modern C++, a language with a rich history dating back to 1981. When combined with compact models and algorithms, this enables researchers to independently implement and execute algorithms across diverse platforms by themselves.

This approach is applicable only when the runtime is short, training algorithms are simple, data formats are straightforward, and Deep Learning models consist of basic components. \libname{BurTorch} suggests that, in certain contexts, implementation and fundamental research can advance simultaneously.


